rmd: honor record_post_sec instead of max(record_post_sec, cooldown_sec)

The motion state machine stopped recording max(record_post_sec, cooldown_sec)
after motion ended, so the WebUI "keep recording after motion" value was
silently overridden whenever cooldown_sec was larger. With the default
cooldown_sec = 10 a user's record_post_sec = 2 produced a ~10s tail (a 2s hand
wave recorded ~18s once the 2s pre-buffer and detector settling are added).

cooldown_sec (re-trigger debounce) and record_post_sec (post-roll) are separate
concepts; conflating them via max() surprises the user. Make record_post_sec
authoritative for the post-roll. record_post_sec also bridges brief motion gaps
(motion resuming within the window returns COOLDOWN -> ACTIVE on the same clip),
which is the behaviour the post-roll value is expected to control anyway.

diff --git a/rmd/rmd_main.c b/rmd/rmd_main.c
index 33a7b1b..227b54c 100644
--- a/rmd/rmd_main.c
+++ b/rmd/rmd_main.c
@@ -123,9 +123,13 @@ static void rmd_update_state(rmd_ctx_t *ctx, bool motion)
 			ctx->last_motion_us = now;
 		} else {
 			int64_t elapsed_sec = (now - ctx->cooldown_start_us) / 1000000;
-			int post_sec = ctx->settings.record_post_sec > ctx->settings.cooldown_sec
-					       ? ctx->settings.record_post_sec
-					       : ctx->settings.cooldown_sec;
+			/* Keep recording for exactly record_post_sec after motion
+			 * stops — the WebUI "keep recording after motion" knob.
+			 * cooldown_sec used to floor this (whichever was larger), so
+			 * a short post silently inherited a long cooldown: a 2s post
+			 * with the default 10s cooldown recorded a 10s tail. The two
+			 * are different ideas; record_post_sec is authoritative. */
+			int post_sec = ctx->settings.record_post_sec;
 			if (elapsed_sec >= post_sec) {
 				ctx->state = RMD_STATE_IDLE;
 
